Z||000017||Songman||06-07-2001||01:24 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by spideyfoot:<BR><B>Hey Aus where do ya get the green needles? Why dont you folks post those image files so everyone can try em.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wouldn't do any good on these four cylinder cars we got unless they could be recalibrated... Those are 120 speedos.<P>By the way.. what is indiglo?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 06-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Taijiguy||06-07-2001||07:42 AM||||Oooooh!Oooooooooh!ooooooooooh!I know this one..."Indiglo" is actually the marketing name Timex uses for the technology used in making the entire watch face light up. The actual technology itself is referred to as electroluminesence. (EL)<BR>It's a flat material made of several layers, including phosphorus, and some conductive materials and some insulators. The whole thing acts sort of like a capacitor, and the excited electrons cause the phosphorus to glow. It's a "cool" light source, as it gives off no heat. It can be cut and shaped like paper, and run through a printer, etc. I''ve been looking into this as well. It's pretty expensive, about $100 for enough raw material to do a Fiero dash not including aux guages.||||reg||4
Z||000019||Uaana||06-07-2001||08:05 AM||||Hmmm..<BR>Since Jasc is one of our clients.....<BR>Hacks?<P>Oh well not my dept..<BR>Just keep in mind you never know who's on these boards and your IP is logged.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Cozmo||06-07-2001||08:07 AM||||Looks really good, Slammed!  EL lighting, you either love it or hate it, the blue color can be rather dazzling at night.  Looking thru the "rice" section at the Zone last week I spotted some flexible rope like lighting (in colors!) about 12" long.  On the non-backlit gauges, maybe you could wrap this stuff around the perimeter of each gauge.  It was cheap, about $12 each.<BR>AusFiero, that panel looks SUPERB!!!  Like Spidey said, how'd you get the green needles?  Did you strip the old orange off and then tint them with transparent color?<BR><B>C02M0</B><BR> ||||reg||14
Z||000021||Taijiguy||06-07-2001||08:13 AM||||It's available in like six different colors, including white, a bluish white, yellow, blue, and a couple of others. The blue is just the most common.||||reg||1
Z||000022||AusFiero||06-07-2001||08:41 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cozmo:<BR><B><BR>AusFiero, that panel looks SUPERB!!!  Like Spidey said, how'd you get the green needles?  Did you strip the old orange off and then tint them with transparent color?<BR>[b]C02M0</B><BR> [/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The needles are pretty easy to do. I scraped the old paint of with a razor and some patience. Then sanded the back surface with 1200 wet and dry sandpaper until the last traces of orange was gone. Then I just sprayed the back surface with 1 coat of fluro green paint. Was the same paint I used to redo inside my gauge panel for the backlighting in green.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
